        "Take an image a CNN classifies correctly with 99% confidence. Add a perturbation so small that every pixel changes by at most 1/255 - invisible to a human, and smaller than JPEG compression noise. The network now says 'gibbon' with 99% confidence. That is an ADVERSARIAL EXAMPLE (Szegedy et al., 2013), and the uncomfortable fact is that these are not rare corner cases you have to hunt for: for a standard model you can find one for essentially ANY input, in a fraction of a second, using the same gradient machinery that trained the network. The direction matters, not the magnitude - a random perturbation of the same size does almost nothing.",
